  "raw_text": "Step 1: Let's denote the payment per dog as \\( p \\).\n\nStep 2: Initially, Denali's total pay is \\( 16p \\) and Nate's total pay is \\( 12p \\).\n\nStep 3: If Denali starts walking \\( 4x \\) more dogs, her new total number of dogs becomes \\( 16 + 4x \\), and her new total pay is \\( (16 + 4x)p \\).\n\nStep 4: If \\( x \\) of Nate's dogs are reassigned to Denali, Denali's new total number of dogs becomes \\( 16 + x \\), and her new total pay is \\( (16 + x)p \\). Nate's new total number of dogs becomes \\( 12 - x \\), and his new total pay is \\( (12 - x)p \\).\n\nStep 5: According to the problem, the ratio of Denali's pay to Nate's pay remains the same under both scenarios. Therefore, we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(16 + 4x)p}{(12 - x)p} = \\frac{(16 + x)p}{12p}\n\\]\n\nStep 6: Simplify the equation by canceling out \\( p \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{16 + 4x}{12 - x} = \\frac{16 + x}{12}\n\\]\n\nStep 7: Cross-multiply to solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n(16 + 4x) \\cdot 12 = (12 - x) \\cdot (16 + x)\n\\]\n\nStep 8: Expand both sides:\n\n\\[\n192 + 48x = 192 + 12x - 16x - x^2\n\\]\n\nStep 9: Simplify the equation:\n\n\\[\n192 + 48x = 192 - 4x - x^2\n\\]\n\nStep 10: Rearrange all terms to one side of the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 52x = 0\n\\]\n\nStep 11: Factor out \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\nx(x + 52) = 0\n\\]\n\nStep 12: Solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\nx = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -52\n\\]\n\nSince \\( x \\neq 0 \\), we have:\n\n\\[\nx = -52\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{-52}<|im_end|>",
